    {
      "id": "reason-bayes",
      "answer": "**≈ 1 %** (about 0.98 %).\n\nWith a prevalence of 1 in 10,000 (0.01 %), a 99 % accurate test (99 % sensitivity, 99 % specificity) produces far more false positives than true positives. In a million people: ~100 have the disease and ~99 test positive; ~999,900 are healthy and ~9,999 test positive. A positive result therefore means ~99 / (99 + 9,999) ≈ 0.98 % chance of actually having the disease — the classic base-rate fallacy.",
      "frames": 155,
      "latencyMs": 43510,
      "costCents": null,
      "textSource": null,
      "error": null
    },
    {
      "id": "reason-tradeoff",
      "answer": "**Recommendation: No, don't add a read replica.**\n\nAt ~30 requests/minute (0.5 QPS), a single Postgres instance — even the smallest managed tier — has orders of magnitude more capacity than you need. A read replica adds operational complexity (replication lag monitoring, connection routing, failover logic) and ongoing cost for zero measurable benefit at this scale.\n\nRevisit only if:\n- Read traffic grows 100×+ (into thousands of QPS)\n- You have long-running analytical queries that block OLTP workloads\n- You need geographic read locality for latency\n\nFor a two-person team, the simpler architecture is the right one.\n\n## Sources\n- [Source](https://servodatabase.com/servo/mpi/mx-30)",
      "frames": 147,
      "latencyMs": 35249,
      "costCents": null,
      "textSource": null,
      "error": null
    },
    {
      "id": "search-current-event",
